course: dictionary
lesson: 8538
title: Who were the Saxons?
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The Saxons were a people from northern Europe who settled in England. Saxon is a noun for a person, as in «the Saxons», and it is also an adjective that describes things connected to them, as in «Saxon villages» or «a Saxon king».
explain: «In the 400s and 500s AD, Angles, Saxons and Jutes settled in Britain.» The clue is settled: it means came to live somewhere and stayed. Angles, Saxons and Jutes arrived together, so the sentence tells you they are a group of peoples.
explain: Saxon has another meaning too. Saxony is a region of Germany, and a Saxon can also mean someone from there. Read the words around the word: if the sentence talks about England or about history, the Anglo-Saxon meaning is the one you need.
fact: The Saxons settled in England from about AD 450, with the Angles and Jutes.
fact: Together, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes are called the Anglo-Saxons.
fact: «Saxon» can mean about these people or their times, as in «Saxon villages».
fact: Old English was spoken from about 450 to 1100 and grew from the speech of these settlers.
fact: Saxony is a region of Germany, and «Saxon» can also mean from there.
exercise: Which people settled in England from about AD 450, with the Angles and Jutes? | *The Saxons | The Normans | The Vikings | The Romans
exercise: «Saxon villages» are a common topic in history lessons. What does Saxon mean here? | A region of France | A kind of sweet bread | *From or about the Saxon people and their times | A large ship with a sail
exercise: Saxony is a region of ___. | *Germany | England | Denmark | France
test: What is the name for the Angles, Saxons and Jutes together? | Romans | Normans | Celts | *Anglo-Saxons
test: Which language grew from the speech of the Angles, Saxons and Jutes? | *Old English | Norse | Welsh | Latin
exam: In the phrase «Saxon king», what kind of word is Saxon? | *An adjective | A noun | A verb | An adverb
